The default order in which topics are presented in the CLL, ''the'' comprehensive Lojban manual/textbook/guide, seems a bit illogical, when first encountered by a beginner. This sparked, in #lojban, a discussion about a more beginner-friendly order, which resembles:

* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/1/1/|Chapter 1] (Legal stuff, skippable) 
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/2/1/|Chapter 2] 
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/3/1/|Chapter 3]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/4/1/|Chapter 4]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/13/1/|Chapter 13.1-13.10]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/6/1/|Chapter 6] (beware of 6.12 (dotside), and 6.2-6.9 (xorlo))
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/7/1/|Chapter 7]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/5/1/|Chapter 5.1-5.7]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/17/1/|Chapter 17.1-17.12]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/19/1/|Chapter 19] (beware of 19.13 (erasure))
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/13/11/|Chapter 13.11-13.16]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/11/1/|Chapter 11]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/8/1/|Chapter 8]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/9/1/|Chapter 9]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/10/1/|Chapter 10]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/14/1/|Chapter 14]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/15/1/|Chapter 15]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/5/10/|Chapter 5.10-5.20]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/12/1/|Chapter 12]
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/16/1/|Chapter 16] (Logical quantifiers, difficult material)
* [http://lojban.github.com/cll/17/13/|Chapter 17.13-17.20]
* ''Not at all necessary'' [http://lojban.github.com/cll/18/1/|Chapter 18] (mekso - may be dropped from the language)
* ''Useful'' [http://lojban.github.com/cll/20/|Chapter 20] (selma'o liste)
* ''Not for the eyes of all'' [http://lojban.github.com/cll/21/1/|Chapter 21] (Formal grammars)

Points containing the mention ''beware'' or ''difficult'' should be read with a grain of salt; investing some time on ((Lojban IRC|Lojban's IRC)) will certainly clear things up, not to mention that Lojbanist irci give lessons in #ckule and (especially in the beginning to work on pronunciation) by means of Mumble, Ventrilo and Skype.